  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Peptides are short chains of amino acids—the building blocks of proteins—that function as critical signaling messengers in the body. They offer benefits like improved wound healing, reduced inflammation, increased muscle mass, and better skin health. While many are used in medical treatments, unapproved, unregulated \"gray market\" peptides pose safety risks.What Are Peptides?Definition: Peptides consist of two or more amino acids linked together, often classified as fragments of proteins.Function: They act as signaling molecules, instructing cells to perform tasks, such as stimulating collagen production or reducing inflammation.How Are Peptides Used in MedicineApproved Therapies: Over 100 peptides are FDA-approved. Examples include insulin for diabetes and GLP-1 drugs like Ozempic/Wegovy for obesity.Therapeutic Potential: Research is ongoing into antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) as alternatives to antibiotics and other specialized therapies for diseases.Controversy: Many popular peptides (e.g., BPC-157, TB-500) are not FDA-approved, are considered \"gray market\" substances, and are banned by international sports agencies as doping agents.Peptide Benefits and UsesSkincare: In skincare, peptides act as carriers, delivering nutrients to skin cells, or as signal peptides that promote collagen and elastin production.Health and Wellness: They are marketed in supplements, powders, and gummies to aid in muscle building, weight loss, and reduced inflammation.Types of PeptidesAntimicrobial Peptides (AMPs): Fight microorganisms and help wound healing.Signaling Peptides: Trigger collagen synthesis to improve skin elasticity and decrease wrinkles.Carrier Peptides: Deliver trace elements like copper to tissues.Hormones: Includes Insulin, Growth Hormone, and GLP-1.Side Effects and RisksSide Effects: Potential side effects from peptide supplements include skin rashes, increased blood pressure, nausea, headaches, and dizziness.Risks: Use of unregulated peptides (especially injectables) can carry risks regarding quality, sterility, and long-term consequences such as receptor downregulation.Disclaimer: Peptides not approved by the FDA are unregulated. It is essential to consult a healthcare provider for any treatment.",
